    "ExplanationCorrect": "Under ASC 820-10-35-37 through 35-54, the fair value hierarchy prioritizes the inputs to valuation techniques into three broad levels. The classification of a fair value measurement in its entirety is determined by the lowest-level input that is significant to the entire measurement. Instrument 1 (CBOE options): Quoted prices in an active market for identical instruments are Level 1 inputs [ASC 820-10-35-40]. The S&P 500 options trade on an active exchange with observable bid-ask prices — this is the classic Level 1 scenario. While implied volatility is used in option pricing, ASC 820-10-35-37A clarifies that a fair value measurement is classified as Level 1 when the measurement is based on a quoted price in an active market for an identical asset, even if the reporting entity then adjusts that price. The key question is whether the implied volatility adjustment is significant to the overall measurement. For exchange-traded options, the primary input is the quoted market price; implied volatility is embedded in that price. The fact that Meridian sources it from Bloomberg does not make it a Level 2 or 3 input — it remains observable market data. Instrument 2 (interest rate swap): Level 2 inputs are inputs other than quoted prices in active markets that are observable, either directly or indirectly [ASC 820-10-35-47]. The SOFR forward curve is directly observable, and the DCF model is a standard valuation technique. The CDS spreads, while from an inactive market, are still market-based observable inputs — they are Level 2, not Level 3. The swap's fair value is driven primarily by the SOFR forward curve (a Level 2 input), and CDS-based credit adjustments are a refinement, not the primary driver. Instrument 3 (NovaTech preferred stock): Level 3 inputs are unobservable inputs for the asset or liability [ASC 820-10-35-52]. Management's revenue growth projections (22-28%), Monte Carlo simulation assumptions, and exit probability modeling are all entity-developed assumptions that are not corroborated by observable market data. The discount rate derived from comparable companies represents an adjustment to observable data that makes it, in practice, a Level 3 input due to the significant entity-specific adjustments (size premium, illiquidity discount). NovaTech's measurement is properly classified as Level 3. A critical exam trap is classifying an entire measurement at Level 1 when a significant input is Level 2 or 3 (or vice versa — classifying at Level 3 when the lowest significant input is actually Level 2). The controller must identify the single lowest-level significant input — not perform a weighted-average or balancing test.",
